00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.000
interact with uv
so you still haven't taken into account&nbsp;

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.800
the whole equation the way that seems to
work the best&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.800
is by making special plates that mimic
human skin and so when the sunscreen&nbsp;

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.040
applies it dries on it just like it
would on skin&nbsp;

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.400
this sounds really obvious and
straightforward but it's really not&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:57.680
scientists have been working on this for
literally decades and it looks like&nbsp;

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.200
there's finally a few methods that might
be good enough to be accepted for spf&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.680
labeling
the best method so far seems to be the&nbsp;

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:07.760
cosmetics europe method
very briefly this uses a robotic arm to&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.040
spread the sunscreen on two different
specially roughened plates&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.080
they take four different uv absorbance
readings and then they put it all into&nbsp;

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:17.600
an equation that calculates the spf
this method is being rolled out and&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.120
hopefully will be accepted in the next
few years but right now it still hasn't&nbsp;

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.080
gotten that official tick of
approval with other in vitro methods&nbsp;

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.360
that use plates there's been a really
big difference between what you see&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.880
on the plate and what you see in vivo on
human skin this graph shows the&nbsp;

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:34.000
correlation and the closer the dot is to
that diagonal line&nbsp;

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.760
the more accurate it is so you can see
some data points that are really far out&nbsp;

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:41.440
here so for example this one on skin was
35.1&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:44.560
in vitro on the plate it was 12. this
one&nbsp;

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:49.040
on skin was 61.2 but the in vitro
prediction predicted 29.&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.120
it also goes the other way so this one
in vivo was 20.3&nbsp;

00:13:53.120 --> 00:14:01.840
but all four methods overestimated it at
31.5 to 42.7&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.560
so clearly testing in a lab without
human skin can be really inaccurate&nbsp;

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.240
what if we test on human skin but with a
simpler method one method i've seen&nbsp;

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:16.480
suggested for checking how much spf you
have is by looking through a uv camera&nbsp;

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:19.600
and seeing how dark it is
i've actually spoken to the maker of one&nbsp;

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.400
of the most popular uv cameras the
sunscreenr&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:26.320
and he says that no you cannot tell the
spf from how dark it is&nbsp;

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.480
the camera is qualitative and not
quantitative it only tells you where you&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.360
spread the sunscreen
and possibly whether it's gotten lighter&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.040
or darker with the same sunscreen
but you can't compare between different&nbsp;

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.920
sunscreens so the darkness can't tell
you if it's spf 15 or spf 100&nbsp;

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.440
part of this is because you can't see
how continuous the film is&nbsp;

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.640
microscopically
so it's far too low resolution for that&nbsp;

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:51.040
it's also to do with how these cameras
work&nbsp;

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:54.720
they work by using specific uv
wavelengths not the entire spectrum of&nbsp;

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.880
erythemal uv which is how we get spf
so if you have a sunscreen that happens&nbsp;

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.000
to be really biased towards that
particular uv wavelength&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.440
then it'll be really dark if you have
one that's particularly not biased&nbsp;

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.840
towards that wavelength it might be
really light&nbsp;

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:11.600
even though overall they end up working
around the same sunscreens that reflect&nbsp;

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.160
or scatter
uv also look a lot lighter even if&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.520
they're only reflecting or scattering a
tiny amount it can still look a lot&nbsp;

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:20.240
lighter on that photo
so the main thing you can tell with&nbsp;

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:23.840
these cameras is just if you've missed a
spot while applying which is exactly&nbsp;

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.840
what these cameras were designed for
[Music]&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.720
so all of these issues with these other
methods is why the in vivo spf test is&nbsp;

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:40.080
still the gold standard this is what's
used for the label so unless you have&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:43.760
evidence that matches up to the in vivo
test that's used for the label&nbsp;

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:47.200
you can't really say very much and we
can see that other tests just don't&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:50.000
really measure up
but there are some issues with this gold&nbsp;

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.560
standard even though it is the best
thing we have&nbsp;

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:57.040
the main test used is the iso two four
four four four spf tests so this was&nbsp;

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.920
where you get human volunteers and burn
them for science&nbsp;

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:03.200
and like i said there are lots of issues
with this like cost and harm&nbsp;

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:07.280
but there is another really big reason
why we're so desperate to replace this&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:11.440
test with an in vitro spf test
and this is reproducibility in other&nbsp;

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:15.280
words testing the same sunscreen using
this method doesn't necessarily give you&nbsp;

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.400
consistent results
the differences between spfs that&nbsp;

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.200
different labs get has always been a
really big issue&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:25.440
so for example procter and gamble sent
the same spf 100 sunscreen to five&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.080
different labs
they told the labs that the spf was&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.000
somewhere between 20 and 100
and they got back five very different&nbsp;

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.160
results between 37
and 75. in another test the five labs&nbsp;

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.320
were sent another sunscreen and they
were told that it was&nbsp;

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.760
spf 80. three labs scored a pretty close
to spf 80.&nbsp;

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:46.720
the two other labs gave at 54 and 70. so
when the labs were told in spf value&nbsp;

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:49.520
they were more likely to get that
expected result and so it looks like&nbsp;

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:53.280
there's some element of bias happening
one thing i did notice in the inci&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:57.200
decoder test was that the lab was told
that the spf would be around 20. i'm&nbsp;

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.640
guessing when purito did their test they
told their lab that it would be higher&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:03.680
and so this extra information might have
influenced the results&nbsp;

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:08.880
so all of this variation is why there's
been a revision to the iso 24444 in 2019&nbsp;

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:11.760
some of the things that are now
specified which could give more accurate&nbsp;

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:15.040
spf results include
the sunscreen has to be applied in drops&nbsp;

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:19.120
so at least 15 drops per
30 square centimeters the drops have to&nbsp;

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:22.320
be spread circularly and then up and
down and then side to side&nbsp;

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:26.160
the process of spreading the sunscreen
on the skin has to be 30 to 40 seconds&nbsp;

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.120
through the whole process the gloved
finger shouldn't leave the skin the&nbsp;

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.480
people who decide if the skin is burnt
or not have to have their colour vision&nbsp;

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.400
checked
and it's recommended that they get&nbsp;

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:38.240
rechecked every year instead of having
an expert classify the skin colour&nbsp;

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.040
there's a limit on the specific skin
colours of volunteers&nbsp;

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.800
and this is measured by a machine and
the average has to be within a certain&nbsp;

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.360
limit
the lamps that are used in uv testing&nbsp;

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:50.560
shine a circle of uv
onto the skin they have to give out the&nbsp;

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:54.000
same amount of uv a consistent amount
over the area of the circle&nbsp;

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.360
so you can see there are a lot of tiny
things in the spf test that could&nbsp;

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.240
influence the result that weren't
actually specified before&nbsp;

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.040
there's also some things that still
aren't standardized and maybe can't be&nbsp;

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.240
standardized so for example the person
spreading the sunscreen&nbsp;

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:10.000
they're meant to use a light pressure
but one person's light might not be&nbsp;

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.440
another person's light and it's pretty
much impossible for the person to use&nbsp;

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.920
the exact same pressure
for every single sample they're&nbsp;

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:19.440
spreading there are a few reference
sunscreens used for spf testing&nbsp;

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:23.440
and the labs use these to see how well
that test was performed for that&nbsp;

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.560
particular batch of people
here's the acceptable variation for each&nbsp;

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:30.640
sunscreen you can see that as you get to
higher spfs the accepted variation gets&nbsp;

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.840
a lot bigger it's actually a lot harder
to measure a higher spf&nbsp;

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.960
with these you can see that the upper
limit is somewhere around double the&nbsp;

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.480
lower limit and remember this is
considered good reproducibility&nbsp;

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.680
now the in vivo tests are pretty much
the same around the world&nbsp;

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:47.360
but one factor that does change is the
types of volunteers used&nbsp;

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.480
in the iso test you're meant to use a
range of different skin types from photo&nbsp;

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:53.600
type 1 to photo type 3.
this is now measured using a machine and&nbsp;

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:56.800
skin color but obviously depending on
the country you're going to get&nbsp;

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:59.280
different volunteers coming in for these
tests&nbsp;

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:02.400
if you look at spf testing data you'll
often see this reflected&nbsp;

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.600
so in asian countries you tend to get a
lot more phototype 3&nbsp;

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:09.120
in western countries you get a lot more
phototype 2 the test procedures are&nbsp;

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.920
slightly different depending on how
prone to burning you are&nbsp;

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:15.840
and so this could influence the final
results this is particularly a problem&nbsp;

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:19.200
if the tester gets the skin type wrong
and this is one of the things that&nbsp;

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.360
machine measurement is meant to help fix
so spf test results from different&nbsp;

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:25.760
countries could be affected by this
difference&nbsp;

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.960
along with the issues with the spf test
there might be differences between&nbsp;

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:32.320
different batches of sunscreen
sunscreen manufacturers usually get that&nbsp;

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:35.360
label test on an early version of the
sunscreen&nbsp;

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:39.280
and they might not re-test it with a
proper in vivo test for many years&nbsp;

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:43.120
small changes in the production of the
sunscreen can also affect the spf value&nbsp;

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.760
there's usually some testing for
consistent composition&nbsp;

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.880
so for example to make sure there's
actually five percent oxybenzone every&nbsp;

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:51.120
time
but again there are lots of other things&nbsp;

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:55.280
in the formula that can change that spf
sunscreens also settle over time so&nbsp;

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:58.960
depending on how fresh the sunscreen is
that can change the spf as well&nbsp;

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:02.720
so with all of these issues with in vivo
spf testing that's why i really think&nbsp;

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:06.000
you do need
two in vivo spf tests to raise&nbsp;

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.720
significant doubt over an spf label
claim&nbsp;

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:12.160
and that's what judit says in the inci
decoder blog post as well&nbsp;

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:15.360
if we only have one then it's really
hard to say whether it's just an issue&nbsp;

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.560
with inter-lab reproducibility
or if there's actually a problem with&nbsp;

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:22.640
the sunscreen i think when you have two
in vivo spf tests that agree and one&nbsp;

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.280
that doesn't
that's when you can confidently say&nbsp;

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:29.920
there probably is a problem somewhere
and the burden of proof switches purito&nbsp;

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.960
posted an update saying they paused the
sales of their sunscreen&nbsp;

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:36.080
and because people had complained to
them earlier about getting burned&nbsp;

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:39.120
they had actually sent their sunscreens
off for extra testing&nbsp;

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:42.480
which i think is the right thing to do
there is still the possibility that the&nbsp;

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:46.000
sample sent to the european labs was a
particularly bad batch&nbsp;

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:49.600
or that the sample sent for the original
testing in the korean lab&nbsp;

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:53.280
was a particularly good batch the
samples for the inci decoder test were&nbsp;

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:56.720
also put into a different plastic
container and then sent off for testing&nbsp;

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:00.960
and so there is a possibility there that
the sunscreen reacted with the container&nbsp;

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:04.480
someone did point out that it says
yellow whereas the purito sunscreen is&nbsp;

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:08.000
actually closer to white
it is really subjective what color is&nbsp;

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:15.840
but that could potentially be a sign of
an issue&nbsp;

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:21.840
so are asian sunscreens less trustworthy
since the purito news came out there's&nbsp;

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:26.080
been a lot of talk of not trusting
korean or asian regulation or testing or&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:28.480
brands
and only trusting larger western&nbsp;

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:31.360
manufacturers but sunscreens getting
tested at&nbsp;

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:35.440
spfs that are lower than their labels has
happened a lot of times before&nbsp;

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.560
there are a number of consumer advocacy
groups around the world&nbsp;

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:42.720
who retest sunscreens using in vivo
methods and a lot of the time they get&nbsp;

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.800
bad results that are lower than the
label&nbsp;

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.080
this happens with western brands
including a lot of larger western brands&nbsp;

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:51.120
who put a lot of effort into making
really good sunscreens&nbsp;

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:54.560
it's worth noting that the methods used
in these consumer tests and in the inci&nbsp;

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:57.680
decoder test
aren't quite the same as the label test&nbsp;

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.080
the biggest difference is the number of
volunteers&nbsp;

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:03.920
for an spf result that goes on the label
the results have to be consistent over&nbsp;

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:07.040
at least 10 volunteers
in the consumer tests they usually just&nbsp;

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:10.640
test on 5 or 10 people and don't talk
about consistency&nbsp;

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:13.840
in one of the inci decoder tests they
only use five results&nbsp;

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:16.640
and in both tests they weren't
consistent enough to be used on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:17.760
an spf
label&nbsp;

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.360
but these tests are in vivo and they
largely use the same procedures&nbsp;

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:25.120
so i think that doesn't affect their
ability to raise doubt over an spf label&nbsp;

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:28.000
claim
so in the u.s consumer reports have&nbsp;

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.520
found that a neutrogena sunscreen and a
la roche-posay sunscreen&nbsp;

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:35.520
both tested at less than half their
labeled spfs the neutrogena one was&nbsp;

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.520
labeled at spf 70 but was tested at spf
30-39&nbsp;

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:44.720
this la roche-posay one was labeled spf
60 but tested at between 10 and 19.&nbsp;

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:50.240
in 2016 consumer reports found that 23
out of the 60 sunscreens they tested&nbsp;

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:54.400
tested at less than half their labeled
spf in october this year the hong kong&nbsp;

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:58.560
consumer council found that 25 out of 30
sunscreens they tested&nbsp;

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:02.240
had less than their spf label and some
of the ones that failed include really&nbsp;

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:08.080
large reputable sunscreen brands so
Estée Lauder, iPSA, Anessa, Shiseido, Curél&nbsp;

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:12.960
Sofina, Laneige, Dermacept and Bio-Essence
one thing that could be contributing to&nbsp;

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.000
why it looks like there's a higher fail
rate for asian sunscreens&nbsp;

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.600
is that the consumer tests were
conducted in an australian lab&nbsp;

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.360
so that ethnicity difference could be an
issue the worst performing sunscreen was&nbsp;

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:25.840
one from fancl which is a japanese
brand&nbsp;

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:29.840
it was labeled spf 50 plus but it tested
out spf 14.3&nbsp;

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:32.880
and the brand claims that the original
label claim was supported by&nbsp;

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:36.320
two different tests from two different
labs in two different countries one from&nbsp;

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:39.600
the us and one from china
i would say that as a brand the best way&nbsp;

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:41.840
of checking that your spf rating was
valid&nbsp;

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:45.280
is to send it to two different labs into
different countries but even though they&nbsp;

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:48.560
did that there was still that
discrepancy with the consumer test&nbsp;

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:52.000
so in this whole purito conversation
some people have recommended that&nbsp;

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:56.000
australian sunscreens are the way to go
now i am australian and i'm very proud&nbsp;

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:59.280
of how my small country is going in
terms of sunscreen regulation&nbsp;

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:03.120
and sunscreen development and skin
cancer research but this still isn't&nbsp;

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:06.880
error proof
in late 2015 choice magazine tested six&nbsp;

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:10.400
spf 50 plus sunscreens that were
available in australia&nbsp;

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.440
and they found that only two of the
sunscreens in the consumer tests&nbsp;

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:17.440
actually met the label claim so this is
not only an issue that you see in korean&nbsp;

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:20.320
sunscreens or asian sunscreens
the fact that this happened with&nbsp;

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:24.640
neutrogena and la roche-posay
two western brands that i consider to be&nbsp;

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:27.360
two of the most trustworthy brands in
sun protection&nbsp;

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.880
they always go above and beyond to
develop high protection sunscreens&nbsp;

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:34.480
i think isn't a sign that these brands
can't be trusted or that any sunscreen&nbsp;

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:36.960
that tests below the label claim must be
lying&nbsp;

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.720
it's a reflection of issues with spf
testing and with batch variations&nbsp;

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:45.360
and in my opinion a reflection of the
robustness of some sunscreens so&nbsp;

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:48.640
in other words how consistently they
perform over time in different&nbsp;

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:50.880
conditions
which isn't really captured by the&nbsp;

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.720
current spf testing
on top of this there's the fact that at&nbsp;

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.680
the end of the day you are still relying
on people to do the right thing&nbsp;

00:24:57.680 --> 00:25:01.120
the biggest sunscreen labeling scandal
that's ever happened is with ama&nbsp;

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:04.560
labs this is a us-based sunscreen
testing lab&nbsp;

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.600
and they're one of the biggest ones
according to the indictment the lab&nbsp;

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:11.600
committed fraud over 30 years between
1987 and 2017.&nbsp;

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:15.760
